1.1.4.m:
For the guise [of sacrifice] I take both of you;
for the rite I take both of you;
for the good deed I take both of you.
O divine waters, moving at the front, leading at the front, go forth at the front of this sacrifice!
Lead the sacrifice to the front, the lord of sacrifice to the front!
You did Indra choose in the contest against Vṛtra;
you chose Indra in the contest against Vṛtra.
You are sprinkled.
May the divine folk settle down together!
You belong to the tree;
you are grown by rain.
Go through the wide atmosphere!
Burnt up is the rakṣas!
Burnt up is the enemy!
You are the yoke-pin;
harm the one who harms, whoever harms us;
whom we harm, harm him!

1.1.8.m:
Burned down is the rakṣas!
Burned down is the enemy!
Do not burn the ocean!
You are fixed;
firm the earth!
O Agni, strike away the flesh-devouring fire;
drive out the feeder on raw flesh!
O Agni, bear the place of sacrifice to the gods!
You are a support;
firm the atmosphere!
You are a supporter;
firm the sky!
You are order;
firm all things!
You are an arrangement;
you are an encircling arrangement;
sit in all the quarters!
Make the kin attend upon this sacrificer;
may the kin attend upon this sacrificer!
Through the heat of the cauldron of the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Bhṛgus, and Aṅgirases, be heated!
The potsherds that the priests arrange at the cauldron,
those, according to Pūṣan's law, let Indra and Vāyu unyoke.

1.1.11.m:
Scorched is the rakṣas!
Scorched is the enemy!
Do not wipe away life [and] breath!
Do not wipe away eye [and] ear!
Do not wipe away voice [and] cattle!
Do not wipe away sacrifice [and] offspring!
You are splendor;
you are brightness;
you are light;
you are nectar;
you belong to all the gods.
You are an offering to Vaiśvānara, of uplifted strength, possessing true power;
you are force;
overcome the enemy, overcome the assailants!
You are thousand-strengthened;
quicken that in me!
You are the clarified butter of clarified butter, the oblation of the oblation, the truth of truth;
besprinkled with truth,
with truth I sprinkle you.
With an undeceived eye I look upon you, for increase of wealth, for good offspring.
You are the dear abode of the gods, the unassailable place of sacrifice;
for the feast of the gods I receive you.
O divine waters, clean, unite with the gods!
Clean, well-attended, may we become your attendants!
You are a black [antelope], abiding in the ditch;
become clarified butter for Agni!
You are the vedi;
acceptable to the barhis I sprinkle you.
You are the barhis;
acceptable to the vedi I sprinkle you.
Svāhā to the Ancestors who drink the cauldron!

1.1.12.m:
May Pūṣan untie your knot!
You are the topknot of Viṣṇu.
Spread wide, soft as wool, a good seat for the gods!
You are the gandharva Viśvāvasu, fleeing from everyone;
the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
You are Indra's right arm;
the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
May Mitra and Varuṇa enclose you from the north;
you are the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
As our constant hotṛ, O wise poet, may we, shining, kindle you!
On the highest summit of the earth, may Sūrya protect you with his rays in front against any curse!
You two are the supports of all people.
You are the seat of the Vasus, Rudras, and Ādityas, the womb of the ladles.
You are heaven by birth, Juhū by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are atmosphere by birth, Upabhṛt by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are earth by birth, Dhruvā by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are the bull, mighty in chant;
I set you in the measure of the vaṣaṭ-call.
They have sat firm in the womb of cosmic order, in the world of good deeds;
guard them, O Viṣṇu!
Guard the sacrifice!
Guard the lord of sacrifice!
Guard me, the leader of the sacrifice!
You are Viṣṇu's;
you are abodes belonging to Viṣṇu, belonging to Prajāpati.

1.2.1.m:
O divine waters, purifying, sweet-laden, cleanse me for the sacrifice to the gods!
O plant, protect this one! O razor, do not harm him!
With that body of yours which is most auspicious, touch him!
I shave these [hairs], listening to the god;
may I attain well-being beyond!
Golden-hued, clean, purifying, the waters have flowed, leaving behind defilement;
a hundred strainers are spread out in them; with these may god Savitṛ purify me!
May the waters, the mothers, refresh me; may the strainers of clarified butter purify me with clarified butter;
for may the divine [waters] carry off all stain; pure, cleansed, I emerge from them!
You are the shelter of Viṣṇu;
grant me shelter!
For vigor [I take] you!
You are the milk of the great ones, the sap of the waters and plants;
you are the bestower of luster;
bestow luster on me!
You are the pupil of Vṛtra's [eye], the bestower of vital strength on my sight;
through you I place between myself and hatred, between myself and enemies, a great mountain;
you are the guardian of sight;
protect my sight!
May the lord of thought purify you!
May the lord of speech purify you!
May the divine Savitṛ purify you with an unbroken strainer, with the rays of the good Sūrya!
O lord of the strainer, with that strainer of yours for whatever purpose I purify myself,
may I accomplish that!

1.2.2.m:
To intention, to the yoker, to Agni, svāhā!
To insight, to the mind, to Agni, svāhā!
To consecration, to ascetic toil, to Agni, svāhā!
To Sarasvatī, to Pūṣan, to Agni, svāhā!
O divine waters, lofty, all-beneficent, heaven and earth, wide atmosphere,
may Bṛhaspati increase us with the oblation, svāhā!
Every mortal would choose the friendship of the divine guide;
everyone begs for wealth; he would choose splendor to flourish, svāhā!
You two are the works of art of the Ṛgveda and Sāmaveda;
I take hold of you both;
protect me until the end!
You are the shelter of Viṣṇu;
grant me shelter;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
Sharpen this vision of the one striving, O god, [sharpen his] determination and skill, O Varuṇa;
by which may we cross over all evils; may I embark upon the boat that brings safe crossing!
O Sūrya and Agni, heaven and earth, wide atmosphere, waters, plants,
O lords of consecration, summon me to consecration!
O divine Savitṛ, you are the lord of consecration of consecration;
protect me as I am thus!
You are vigor, of the Aṅgirases, soft as wool;
place vigor in me!
You are the womb of Indra;
protect me;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
I plow up agriculture yielding good grain; make the plants rich in fruit!
O horn, untie this knot which is tangled in his heart,
the mind of his which is tangled!
You are great, of the trees;
rich in splendor, bestow splendor upon the sacrificer;
protect me from the sight of the constellations!
We ask of you, O gods, what is desirable as the sacrificial rite proceeds;
for in your invocation, O gods worthy of sacrifice, we call upon you.
Svāhā, from the mind the sacrifice,
svāhā, from heaven,
svāhā, from the earth,
svāhā, from the wide atmosphere,
svāhā, from the wind I seize [it], svāhā!

1.2.3.m:
May I not be parted from the presence of the constellations!
Perform the vow!
We contemplate the divine vision, very gracious for our assistance,
bestowing luster, conveying the sacrifice; may it be easily approachable under our control!
Those gods born of Manu, yoked by the mind, of good skill, whose father is Dakṣa, may they help us, may they protect us!
To them, svāhā!
Auspicious, when drunk, be you, O waters, very friendly in our womb, in our belly;
providing refreshment, free from disease, guiltless, auspicious, be you for our life!
O desire of the oblations, most gladdening, O Agni, do you keep good watch; may we rejoice well!
Protect us for welfare; awaken us again!
Again mind, again life has come to us; again breath, again intention has come;
may Vaiśvānara, undeceived, protector of the body, ward off all evils!
You, O Agni, are the protector of vows, a god among mortals;
you are to be praised at sacrifices; maintain the vow in us!
Pūṣan [is the lord] of acquisitions;
Soma [is the lord] of bounties;
may the one who fulfills not be deprived of fulfillment,
nor we of life and of luster!
Give so much, O Soma;
bring yet more!
God Savitṛ is the giver of wealth from the good;
Vāyu is the guardian;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the overlord;
Pūṣan is the receiver.
O divine waters, O child of the waters, that wave of yours which is fit for oblation, full of strength, most intoxicating, may I not step upon it!
Those voices which are in the bull of the cattle—Sūrya was foremost, Śukra was foremost—those I send forth according to your portion here;
may the voices return auspicious to us!
For Vāyu, you!
For Varuṇa, you!
For Rudra, you!
For Nirṛti, you!
For Indra, you!
For the Maruts, you!

1.2.4.m:
This is your bright body, this is your luster; unite with it;
attain brilliance!
You are impetuous, held by the mind, pleasing to Viṣṇu;
in the impulsion of you whose power is true, may I attain the harness of the body, svāhā!
You are bright;
you are shining;
you are nectar;
you belong to all the gods;
you are thought;
you are zeal;
you are vision;
you are the sacrificial fee;
you are worthy of sacrifice;
you belong to the ruling power;
you are Aditi, facing both ways;
as such, be well-facing-forward, well-facing-backward to us!
May Mitra bind you by the foot!
May Pūṣan protect the paths!
For Indra, the overseer!
May your mother consent to you, may your father [consent],
may your brother of the same womb [consent],
may your companion of the same herd [consent]!
As such, O goddess, go to the god;
to Indra, [bring] Soma!
May Rudra turn you back;
safely, with Soma as your companion, come again!
You are a Vasu;
you are a Rudra;
you are Aditi;
you are an Āditya;
you are shining;
you are a Rudra;
may Bṛhaspati delight you in favor!
Rudra has taken notice [of you] together with the Vasus.
Upon the head of the earth I sprinkle you, worthy of sacrifice, in the footprint of Iḍā, full of clarified butter, svāhā!
Delight among us!
In us be your riches;
yours be the riches;
yours, yours be the riches;
may we not be separated from the flourishing of wealth!
Accompanied by Tvaṣṭṛ, may we attend upon you!

1.2.6.m:
You are self-born;
you are self-become;
[you are] born for our ritual action;
by truth I grasp you;
by truth protect us!
Come to us, O Mitra, bestower of good friends, together with increase of wealth!
Enter the right thigh of Indra, willing, into willing, gracious into gracious!
Upward with life, with good life, upward with the sap of the plants,
upward from the realm of Parjanya, I have arisen following immortality!
Traverse the wide atmosphere!
Sit on the seat of Aditi!
The bull propped heaven; he measured out the wide space of the atmosphere, the expanse of the earth;
he sat down as universal ruler over all beings; all these are the vows of Varuṇa.
He spread the atmosphere out among the trees, swift vigor in racers, milk in inviolable [cows],
wisdom in hearts, Varuṇa [placed] fire in the directions, the sun in heaven; he placed Soma on the rock.
You are the yoke-pin;
injure the injurer who would injure us!
Whom we would injure, injure him!
You belong to Varuṇa;
may Varuṇa prop you up!
You are the prop of Varuṇa!
Move forward, O lord of the world, toward all domains!
May no thieves, may no waylayers, may no evil wolves find you!
Becoming a falcon, fly away
to the house of our sacrificer, consecrated
for the pressing for the gods!
Homage to the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa! Serve that truth for the great god!
Praise the sun, far-seeing, born of the gods, the banner, the son of heaven!
You belong to Varuṇa;
may Varuṇa prop you up!
You are the releasing of Varuṇa’s prop!
Untied is the fetter of Varuṇa;
flung away is the fetter of Varuṇa;
homage to the fetter of Varuṇa!
You are the body of Agni;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You are the body of Soma;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You are the hospitality of the guest;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
For Agni the giver of increase of wealth,
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
For the falcon who brought Soma,
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You belong to Varuṇa;
you are Varuṇa whose vow is firm;
sit on the seat of truth of Varuṇa;
for Varuṇa [I take] you!

1.2.7.m:
You are the birthplace of Agni;
you two are the two bulls;
you are Urvaśī;
you are Āyu;
you are Purūravas;
you are the Gāyatrī;
you are the Triṣṭubh;
you are the Jagatī!
Be of one mind, of the same dwelling, like-minded, spotless toward us!
Do not harm the sacrifice, do not harm the lord of the sacrifice, O Jātavedases;
be auspicious to us today!
Fire enters, moving within fire, the son of the ṛṣis, this sovereign ruler.
Let us serve him with an oblation; may we not disturb the share of the gods!
For the lord of falling upon, I take you;
for the lord of flying around, I take you;
for the son of the body, capable, mighty, most powerful through ability!
Unassailable are you, unassailable might of the gods, protecting against accusation, not to be accused!
May the lord of vows approve this vow of mine; may the lord of consecration approve the consecration!
I have straightway reached the truth;
set me upon prosperity!
O Agni, lord of vows, whatever body is mine, that is in you;
O Agni, lord of vows, whatever body is yours, this is in me;
together, O lord of vows, are the vows of us two vow-holders!
May stalk after stalk swell for you, O divine Soma, for Indra who wins the single prize!
Swell for Indra!
May Indra swell for you!
Make your comrades swell with acquisition, with wisdom!
Safely, O divine Soma, may I reach your pressing, safely the conclusion!
Sought is wealth, sought are lovely things, forth for vigor, for Bhaga;
truth to those who speak truth!
Homage to heaven,
homage to earth!
O Agni, that body of yours which lies in metal, the highest, dwelling in the depth,
you have struck down vehement speech, you have struck down fierce speech, svāhā!
That [body] of yours, O Agni, which lies in dust;
that [body] of yours, O Agni, which lies in heat;
that body of yours, O Agni, which belongs to Rudra—protect us with it; to that of yours, svāhā!

1.2.8.m:
You are my course of austerity;
you are my course of finding [wealth].
May it protect me, the supplicant;
may it protect [me], the afflicted.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon this earth, come with the name of life!
May the Vasus bring you with the Gāyatrī metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon the second earth, come with the name of life!
May the Rudras bring you with the Traiṣṭubha metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon the third earth, come with the name of life!
May the Ādityas bring you with the Jāgata metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
May you find, O Agni, that cloud-name which is yours.
You are a lioness;
you are a buffalo cow;
purify yourself for the gods;
adorn yourself for the gods!
May the Indraghoṣas protect you from the east with the Vasus;
may the swift-minded fathers protect you from the south;
may the wise Rudras protect you from the west;
may Viśvakarman protect you from the north with the Ādityas!
You are a lioness, overcoming rivals, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning increase of wealth, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning good offspring, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning the Ādityas, winning kinfolk, svāhā!
You are a lioness!
Bring hither the gods for the sacrificer who serves the gods, svāhā!
To the beings, [I offer] you!
You possess all life;
make the earth firm!
You are a firm dwelling;
make the atmosphere firm!
You are an unshakeable dweller;
make the heaven firm!
You are the ashes of Agni;
you are the rubble of Agni;
you are the nest of Agni!
May the gleaming, high one drink the sweet honey of Soma, bestowing unswerving life upon the lord of the sacrifice;
he who, urged by the wind, guards by his own nature, delivers the offspring, and shines forth in manifold ways.

1.3.30.m:
You are taken with a support.
You are bay, harnesser of the bays, possessor of bays, standing on the two bays;
of you whose praise is sung, O divine Soma, whose chant is recited, whose Yajus is offered,
of the possessor of bays, of the harnesser of the bays, may I obtain the cup with bays!
Roasted barley of the two bays, rich in bays, accompanied by Soma, for Indra;
for wealth, you;
for prosperity, you!

1.3.36.m:
May Agni from the morning pressing protect us, Vaiśvānara, sharing all splendor, bringing well-being to all;
may he, the purifier, bestow wealth upon us; possessing a full life-span, may we share the feast!
For Agni I pluck you out with the Gāyatrī metre;
for Indra I pluck you out with the Triṣṭubh metre;
for the Ādityas I pluck you out with the Jagatī metre;
in the flight of the roaring ones I shake you;
in the flight of the exhilarated ones I shake you;
in the flight of the rejoicing ones I shake you;
in the flight of the purified ones I shake you;
in the flight of the homestead-dwellers I shake you;
in the flight of the honeyed ones I shake you;
in the flight of the sweet-flowing ones I shake you;
in the flight of the god-traversed ones I shake you.
You are taken with a support.
You, bright, O Śukra, for Śukra I take, in the form of day, in the rays of the sun.
They have shaken from the ocean; the streams of heaven have not ceased.
The lofty form of the bull shines on high,
in the thought of the Vasus, in the work of the Rudras, in the mind of the Ādityas;
for Indra possessing extensive wealth I offer you.
Desiring, O divine Soma, with the Gāyatrī metre approach the domain of Agni;
controlling, O divine Soma, with the Triṣṭubh metre approach the domain of Indra;
as our friend, O divine Soma, with the Jagatī metre approach the dear path of the All-Gods!

1.3.37.m:
Upward carry the banners that god who knows all beings,
Sūrya, for all to see.
The bright face of the gods has arisen, the eye of Mitra, of Varuṇa, of Agni;
he has filled heaven and earth and the atmosphere, Sūrya, the self of the moving and the standing.
Go to heaven!
Go to the sun!
I approach your form with form, [your] vigor with vigor;
may Tutha, all-knowing, distribute you on the highest firmament of the earth!
This gift of yours, O Agni, goes forth, dropped from Soma;
lead it along the path of Mitra!
May this Agni make broad space for us; may he go forward, cleaving the assailants;
may he conquer the enemies, exulting; may he win the prize in the contest for prizes!
Go forth along the path of sacred order, having shining sacrificial gifts;
today may I gratify a Brahmin who has ancestors, of ancestral lineage, a seer descended from seers, having goodly sacrificial gifts!
Look at the sun! Look at the atmosphere!
Strive together with those sitting in the Sadas!
Given by us, full of sweetness, go to the gods!
Enter the giver!
Without forsaking us, by the path traversed by gods, sit in the world of the righteous;
that has been prepared for us!

1.4.7.m:
'You are real; may I become real!'—
these are indeed blessings;
them he secures.
'You are full; may I become full!'—
full with limbs, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'You are complete; may I become complete!'—
complete with limbs, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'You are imperishable; may I become imperishable!'—
imperishable, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'May the divine officiating priests cleanse [the sacrifice] through the eastern quarter!'—
these, indeed, are the cleansing rites of the sacrifice;
these are the propitiations.
The Baijavāpas knew them;
their sacrifice became cleansed, propitiated, [and] Paśupati did not slay their cattle.
Therefore, he who knows thus—his sacrifice becomes cleansed, propitiated, and Paśupati does not slay his cattle.
'Viṣṇu strode across the earth with the gāyatrī metre,' [he says].
With Viṣṇu at their head, indeed, the gods, having driven the asuras from these worlds, went to the heavenly world.
So the sacrificer, with Viṣṇu at his head, having driven his rival from these worlds, goes to the heavenly world.
'We have gone to the light,' [he says];
he goes to the heavenly world itself.
'We have become united with the light,' [he says];
for the heavenly world is light.
'Here do I wrap up the breath of so-and-so,' [he says];
he wraps up his breath itself.
He turns around in this manner;
for in this manner does the sacrifice turn around.
Moreover, he turns around following the course of that sun.
'You are splendour,' he says;
for Agni is splendour.
He, indeed, having left behind both offspring and cattle, goes to heaven.
When he says:
'May I be united with offspring, offspring with me; may I be united with cattle, cattle with me,'
he establishes himself firmly in offspring and in cattle.
'O Agni, lord of the house, may I, through you as lord of the house, be a good householder;
may you, through me as lord of the house, be a good householder,' [he says];
they grow old together without mutual seizure;
they attain the full life-span;
they do not incur distress.
He speaks the name of his son;
he continues the line of offspring itself.
He turns toward the human;
for he speaks the name of a human.
When he says:
'Unbroken is the divine thread; let not the human thread be broken,'
he joins together both the divine and the human.
'May I not be cut off from the divine domain, nor from the human,' [he says];
both conquer this world;
they are together in the heavenly world.

1.4.8.m:
[Saying] thus,
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers on the earth, he ascended to the intermediate space;
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers in the intermediate space, he went to heaven;
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers in heaven, he established him in preeminence among his kin.
The sacrificial cake is indeed the head of the sacrifice, the veda [grass-bundle] is the hair.
When he sweeps the sacrificial cake with the veda,
[it is] for the completeness of the sacrifice,
and also for its sacrificial fitness.
The sacrifice, indeed, concealed itself from the gods;
the gods found it by means of the veda.
That is the veda-nature of the veda.
When he sits on the altar with the veda,
he finds the sacrifice for him.
He gives the veda to the wife;
hard to find, indeed, is the sacrifice yonder;
he finds the sacrifice for him.
He gives it three times;
for the gods have a threefold truth.
It is placed in her lap;
she becomes prone to bear a male.
The veda is cattle;
for it is plants.
This space between the two fires is indeed the world of cattle;
in his own world the sacrificer deprives his rival of cattle.
Half-month by half-month the sacrifice is interrupted.
He goes strewing continuously from the Āhavanīya fire,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
To it, thus made continuous, he sacrifices in the following half-month.
'Possessing cows, O Agni, possessing sheep, possessing horses is the sacrifice,' [he says];
he has made the sacrifice rich in cattle, rich in sheep, rich in horses.
'Rich in food is this, O Asura, rich in offspring, long-lasting wealth, broad-based, possessing an assembly,' [he says];
he asks for a blessing.
'May the wife go together with the husband among the well-doers,' [he says];
this is the wife's holding on to the sacrifice;
they are together in the heavenly world.
She who is his wife sits behind facing directly toward him;
if she were to sit behind facing directly toward [the fire], it would burn up her offspring.
When he says:
'O wife, this is your place,'
he makes a place for her;
it does not burn up her offspring.
'Which Sarasvatī is the house-gatherer,' [he says];
[she is] the house-gatherer;
he grants him settlements thereby.
Moreover, his kin, subsisting [on him], wait upon him, [saying]:
'Let us wait upon the voice of this brāhmaṇa or rājanya.'
They should not cook meat on that fire;
if they were to cook, they would make it a corpse-eater;
for they do not cook meat on that fire in which they offer oblations.
'You are unassailable, O Agni, and blameless,' [he says];
'unassailable' is indeed the dear body of Agni.
Indeed, they say: 'by the steadfastness of the custom-guardian.'
Both what is done here in the sacrifice and what is not,
and what expiation of the sacrifice we know here and what we do not,
of both this is the expiation.

1.4.11.m:
The theologians say:
'They sprinkle the kindling; they sprinkle the oblation.
The kindling is the first of the oblations.
Why are there offering and invitatory verses for the other oblations,
[and] why not for the kindling?'
'Recite for Agni as he is being kindled!'—
[this is] the invitatory verse; the sāmidhenī verses are the offering verse; the invitation is the vaṣaṭ-call.
Where too much is done at the sacrifice, there too much is produced in the sacrificer's self;
if [it is] not attained, the sacrifice is cut off, [and] the sacrificer becomes hungry.
With the concluding verse of the sāmidhenī verses he completes the last [log] of the kindling;
the sacrificer does not become hungry;
too much is not produced in his self.
He who knows Prajāpati as seventeenfold, connected with the sacrifice, his sacrifice does not falter; he is established in Prajāpati through the sacrifice.
'Make [him] hear!'—
four syllables;
'Let it be heard!'—
four syllables;
'We who sacrifice'—
five syllables;
the vaṣaṭ-call has two syllables;
this indeed is Prajāpati, the seventeenfold, connected with the sacrifice.
Whether he exceeds the invitatory verse or the offering verse, if he does not depart from this, his sacrifice does not falter; he is established in Prajāpati through the sacrifice.
'Indeed we do not know whether we are Brahmins or non-Brahmins, whether we are of this ṛṣi or of another, whose [name] we pronounce;
but whoever pronouncing [the ancestry] sacrifices, to him that sacrifice comes;
it does not fall to another.'
Therefore, when the pravara is being chosen, he should say:
'O divine fathers, O fatherly gods,
whoever I am, being he, I sacrifice;
whoever I am, being he, I act;
may my sacrifice be prosperous, prosperous [and] appeased, prosperous [and] accomplished!'
Then whoever he is, being he, he sacrifices; to him that sacrifice comes;
it does not fall to another.
'With the measure, the counter-measure, the perimeter-measure, the upward-measure of the sacrifice I encompass you'—
the Gāyatrī is indeed the measure of the sacrifice,
the Triṣṭubh the counter-measure,
the Jagatī the perimeter-measure,
the Anuṣṭubh the upward-measure;
these metres indeed carry the sacrifice;
with them he encompasses it.

1.4.12.m:
The Gandharvas and Apsarases asked Keśin Dārbhya:
'How is it that you, a sacrificer, are equal to a sacrificer who is your rival?'
'I know,' he said.
They said:
'Come, explain the sacrifice to us!'
He explained the sacrifice to them.
They said:
'Even by this the sacrificer would be worse off than his rival sacrificer.'
They said:
'We will arrange the sacrifice for you in such a way that you, a sacrificer, will overcome the rival sacrificer.'
They arranged the oblations, the sacrifice, for him.
Then Keśin overcame Śaṇḍika Audbhāri.
He overcomes his rival through the sacrifice who knows thus.
The āghāra [libation] is the breath;
it is to be offered in the eastern half;
he places breath in his mouth.
The āghāra is the breath;
it is to be offered in the middle;
he places breath in his middle.
He should offer the āghāra as the most abundant of the oblations;
the āghāra is the breath;
he makes his breath the most abundant.
He should pour the āghāra continuously;
the āghāra is the breath;
for the continuity of breath.
He should pour the āghāra upward for one desiring heaven;
the āghāra is the sacrificer;
he makes the sacrificer go to the heavenly world.
For one whom he hates, he should pour it downward;
he becomes worse off.
One oblation steps forward;
one steps away;
one is established.
That which he offers stepping forward, that is stepping forward;
that which he offers stepping away, that is stepping away;
that which he offers standing in the same place, that is established.
For him whom he may wish, 'May he become further wealthier, better,' he should offer stepping forward;
thereby he becomes further wealthier, better.
Then for him whom he may wish, 'May he become further worse off,' he should offer stepping away;
thereby he becomes further worse off.
Then for him whom he may wish, 'May he be neither wealthier nor worse off,' he should offer standing in the same place;
thereby he becomes neither wealthier nor worse off.
The fore-offerings are the seasons;
they are to be offered in the same place;
for the firm establishment of the seasons.
That [portion] which is of Agni's ghee-portion is to be offered in the northern half;
that which is further north than it belongs to the Rakṣases;
that which is of Soma's ghee-portion is to be offered in the southern half;
that which is further south than it belongs to the fathers.
This is the space between the oblations;
arranged are his oblations offered in proper sequence, who knows thus.
He who offers in the smoke offers it in darkness;
then the sacrificer becomes unpleasing to look at.
That which he offers on the coals is a blind oblation;
then the eye of the sacrificer is prone to perish.
Both are to be offered in what possesses light;
the sacrificer becomes pleasing to look at;
his eye does not perish.
That which a non-Brahmin eats is a spilled oblation;
of that Vasiṣṭha alone knew the expiation.
'Protect, O bright one!'—with this he should touch the sacrificial cake;
'Let the shareholder have his share!
May the shareless not have a share!
This is the oblation of the Brahmins, Soma-worthy, Soma-drinkers;
there is no part here for a non-Brahmin.
For me, acting, may it not diminish;
for me, giving, may it not fail!'
He establishes the sacrifice among those worthy of sacrificial gifts, unspilled, undisturbed;
and even that which a non-Brahmin eats becomes offered for him.

1.4.14.m:
One intending to undertake the new- and full-moon sacrifices should pour out [a cake] on eleven potsherds for Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
Having taken hold of the deities and the sacrifice, he undertakes the new- and full-moon sacrifices.
Some also offer the Jaya [oblations].
The gods and the Asuras contended.
Prajāpati saw these Jaya [oblations];
he gave them to Indra.
With them he conquered the Asuras firm step by firm step.
Firm step by firm step he conquers his rival for whom these are offered.
'Intention and impulse,' [he says];
intention is the sacrifice;
impulse is the sacrificial gift.
'Thought and thinking,' [he says];
mind is thought;
speech is thinking.
'Contemplation and study,' [he says];
offspring is contemplation;
cattle is study.
'Known and knowledge,' [he says];
the ṛc is the known;
the sāman is knowledge.
'Bhaga and insight,' [he says];
Prajāpati is Bhaga;
the sacrifice is insight.
'New moon and full moon,' [he says];
[they are] the new- and full-moon [sacrifices] themselves.
That makes twelve;
twelve months are the year;
having reached the year, he secures it.
'Prajāpati gave the Jaya [oblations] to mighty Indra, fierce, victorious in battles.
Through them, racing for the prize, may we win the prize; through them may we overcome all armies'—[with this verse]
he should offer the thirteenth oblation.
There is a thirteenth month;
having reached it with this [oblation], he secures it.
'O Agni, giver of strength, give force and vigor to me as I stride forward, who work curses [against enemies] and am free from curse;
for pre-eminence in this community, svāhā!'—with this he should sacrifice when he desires, 'May I be distinguished in this community.'
Distinguished indeed he becomes in that community, but a variegated mark is born on his body.

1.5.2.m:
You, O Agni, have the splendor of the sun;
unite me with lifespan, with splendor.
You, O Agni, have united with the light of the sun,
with the praise of the ṛṣis, with the dear abode.
May I unite with lifespan, with splendor, with offspring, with the prosperity of wealth.
Kindling you for a hundred winters, radiant, may we kindle you,
possessed of vitality, [you] who grant vitality, possessed of power, [you] who grant power,
O Agni, crusher of rivals, having good heroes, [you] the unassailable.
You are the kindling stick of Agni;
protect me from slander.
You are the kindling stick of Soma;
be a supreme protector to me.
You are the kindling stick of Yama;
protect me from death.
You are the placer of lifespan, O Agni;
place lifespan in me.
You are the placer of splendor, O Agni;
place splendor in me.
You are the protector of sight, O Agni;
protect my sight.
You are the protector of hearing, O Agni;
protect my hearing.
You are the protector of the body, O Agni;
protect my body.
Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me.
O Agni, with whatever heat you have, burn against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever glowing flame you have, glow against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever radiant beam you have, beam against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever fiery force you have, strike against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever brilliance you have, sharpen against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, lord of radiant lusters, homage to your radiant luster;
place radiant luster in me.
O you whose wealth is wondrous, may I attain your far shore in safety.
O you whose wealth is hither, may I attain your far shore in safety.
You are waters;
may I enjoy your waters.
You are greatness;
may I enjoy your greatness.
You are vigor;
may I enjoy your vigor.
You are prosperity of wealth;
may I enjoy your prosperity of wealth.
O wealthy ones, rest in this womb, in this cattle-pen;
this is your relative;
do not go away from here;
become many;
do not abandon me.
You are united, all-shaped;
enter me with vigor, with lordship over cattle, with offspring, with prosperity of wealth.
May your riches rest in me;
may I attain a thousandfold prosperity of you.

1.5.4.m:
Great be the celestial aid of the three, Mitra and Aryaman, / unassailable [that] of Varuṇa.
For when they are at home, or on travels, or in perils, / no deceitful, malicious slanderer has power over them.
For they, the sons of Aditi, bestow shelter continuously, / excellent things to the worshipper.
Make, O Brahmaṇaspati, the Soma-presser famed, / [like] Kakṣīvant, who is the son of Uśij.
He who is wealthy, who slays disease, finds wealth, increases nourishment, / may he, who is auspicious, attend us.
Of Mitra, the sustainer of peoples, the god, the fame is winning,
splendor most wondrous in renown.
You are never barren;
you never become negligent.
May your unassailable chariot encompass us from every side, / with which you protect the worshippers.
You are the crusher;
may I crush down him who hates us and whom we hate.
You are the overcomer;
may I overcome him who hates us and whom we hate.
You are the preeminent;
may I excel and surpass him who hates us and whom we hate.
May Pūṣan, protector of the path, protect me;
may Pūṣan, protector of cattle, protect me;
may Pūṣan, overlord, protect me.
The eastern quarter has Agni as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Agni.
The southern quarter has Indra as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Indra.
The western quarter has the Maruts as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur the Maruts.
The northern quarter has Mitrā-Varuṇā as deities;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Mitrā-Varuṇā.
The upward quarter has Soma as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Soma.
May order protect me from unrighteousness;
may dispensation protect me from violation.
Lifespan and extended life, and sight and broad sight, and forward-breath and outward-breath.
O Uruka, by the speech of you, Uruka, may we attain shared food, O Agni, lord of the house.

1.5.5.m:
For whomsoever in the Agnihotra the stoma is yoked, heaven becomes his.
It is indeed a non-sacrifice where the stoma is not yoked.
[With] "Approaching the sacrifice":
this [earth] indeed is approaching (úpotis);
from here indeed he yokes the Soma;
moreover, he joins this [earth] to the stoma;
moreover, whatever creatures have come to be having names, them indeed he joins to the stoma.
[With] "Following his ancient gleam":
yonder world indeed is the ancient;
from yonder indeed he yokes the stoma;
moreover, the gods indeed are the ancient;
them indeed he joins to the stoma.
From both sides indeed he yokes the stoma, both from here and from yonder.
Having yoked the gods, he goes to the heavenly world.
When he says:
"Near, near, now, O generous one, more, now, those":
this [earth] indeed is approaching;
upon this [earth] indeed he becomes established.
Then, when he utters a verse containing *upa*:
whatever creatures are about to come to be, them indeed he joins to the stoma.
[With] "May your unassailable chariot":
from both sides indeed with this [verse] he encompasses the stoma yoked, both from here and from yonder.
[With] "Agni is the head":
heavenly thereby [it becomes];
[with] "The summit of heaven":
heavenly thereby [it becomes];
[with] "This lord of the earth":
a couple thereby [it becomes];
[with] "He quickens the seed of the waters":
it is full of seed, bringing cattle, completely prosperous.
He stands by with a gāyatrī [verse];
for Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī, having the gāyatrī metre;
with his own metre indeed he stands by him.
[With] "Both of you, Indra and Agni, are to be invoked":
for both of these [go] together;
for this [Agni] enters yonder [sun] when it has become day;
therefore yonder one shines by day;
yonder one enters this [earth/Agni] by night;
therefore this one [shines] by night.
When he says, "Both of you":
he stands by both of them without interruption;
he shines in both worlds, in this one and in yonder.
He stands by with a triṣṭubh [verse].
[With] "He was established here first by the creators":
for Agni was established first upon this [earth];
[with] "The Hotṛ, most worthy of sacrifice, to be praised at the rites":
for this one is the Hotṛ, most worthy of sacrifice, to be praised at the rites;
[with] "Whom Apnavāna and the Bhṛgus made shine forth":
for Apnavāna and the Bhṛgus made him shine forth;
[with] "In the woods, wondrous, pervasive for every clan":
for he pervades all this.
He stands by with a jagatī [verse].
[With] "Following his ancient gleam":
the heavenly world indeed is the ancient;
he becomes established in the heavenly world.
[With] "This is your seasonal womb":
for this is his seasonal womb,
Agni [is the womb] of the sun.
He stands by with an anuṣṭubh [verse].

1.5.6.m:
[With] "Approaching the sacrifice",
he sows [seed] indeed with this [verse].
[With] "Agni is the head",
in that which has been sown he places the seed with this [verse].
[With] "Both of you, Indra and Agni, are to be invoked":
Indra and Agni indeed are the in-breath and out-breath;
the in-breath and out-breath indeed are thus placed at the head of the sacrifice.
[With] "He was established here first by the creators",
he placed the embryo indeed.
[With] "Following his ancient gleam",
he made the udder indeed.
[With] "This is your seasonal womb",
he caused [it] to be born and he made [it] grow.
He stands by with six [verses];
six indeed are the pṛṣṭhas;
he arranges the pṛṣṭhas indeed.
[With] "I have praised Dadhikrāvan", he stands by with [a verse] addressed to Dadhikrā;
this indeed is Agni's dear body addressed to Dadhikrā, bringing cattle, completely prosperous.
Unto Agni's dear abode indeed he approaches with this [verse];
moreover, he becomes rich in cattle;
moreover, the sacrificer purifies himself with this [verse].
He stands by with seven [verses];
seven-footed is the Śakvarī;
cattle are of the Śakvarī;
he secures cattle indeed.
When established, this one ages indeed;
for Agni is a beast;
therefore one should offer these oblations of the fire-establishment year by year;
thereby he does not age;
thereby he makes him renewed.
That is not to be heeded.
He is to be stood by with these very [verses] addressed to Agni Pavamāna, which are the offering verse and invitatory verse of the fire-establishment;
thereby he does not age;
thereby he makes him renewed.
He stands by with twelve [verses];
twelve months are a year;
having attained the year, he secures it.
With [a verse] addressed to Agni and Soma he is to be stood by as the thirteenth;
there is a thirteenth month;
having attained it with this [verse], he secures it.

1.5.7.m:
The theologians say:
"Why do they attend upon the fire in the evening, [and] why not in the morning?"
Yonder sun indeed sets in motion in the evening;
therefore they attend upon [it] in the evening.
This one sets in motion in the morning;
therefore they do not attend upon [it] in the morning.
Therefore in the evening, when the Agnihotra is unoffered, the Agnihotrin must not eat;
and therefore in the morning, when [it is] unoffered, he must not eat.
Therefore in the evening [people are] hostile to a guest;
but on account of merit they give in the morning.
With the morning hand-washing [the fire] is to be attended upon in the morning.
While [the milk] is put on [the fire] or while it is being drawn up, he should wash his hands;
there he should speak four verses of the Vihavya [hymn], [and] four at the morning hand-washing.
By the morning hand-washing, truly, he reaches what has not been reached, he secures what has not been secured;
what has not been reached he indeed reaches by this, what has not been secured he secures.
This [fire], truly, was afraid when brought forth;
the gods surrounded it with the metres.
When he attends upon [it],
he surrounds it with the metres indeed.
He has done both for him.
With a verse addressed to Agni and Soma [the fire] is to be attended upon during the first half-month;
the first half-month, truly, belongs to Agni and Soma;
he hands it over to the second half-month indeed.
With a verse addressed to Indra and Agni it is to be attended upon during the second half-month;
the second half-month, truly, belongs to Indra and Agni;
he hands it over to the first half-month indeed.
All the deities indeed, having passed [him] along, protect him without looking away, who, knowing thus, attends upon the fire.

1.5.8.m:
[With] "You, O Agni, are sun-splendored", he prays for a blessing for greater excellence, for greater welfare.
[With] "Unite me with life, with splendor", he prays for a blessing for himself.
[With] "You, O Agni, have joined with the light of the sun":
for together at that time do these two lights become.
[With] "With the praise of the ṛṣis":
the metres indeed are the praise of the ṛṣis;
he prospers it with the metres indeed.
[With] "With [your] dear abode":
the oblations indeed are the dear abode of Agni;
he prospers it with the oblations indeed.
[With] "May I be joined with life, with splendor, with offspring, with increase of wealth":
he prays for a blessing indeed.
[With] "Kindling you for a hundred winters":
for the invocation is an army-conquering one;
with it a noble should attend upon [the fire];
for when a noble conquers armies, then he prospers;
and one who is not a noble may attend upon [it];
for everyone wishes to conquer armies, everyone wishes to thrive.
Manu truly had ten wives:
having ten sons, having nine sons, having eight sons, having seven sons, having six sons, having five sons, having four sons, having three sons, having two sons, having one son.
Those who were nine, one went over to them;
those who were eight, two [went over] to them;
those who were seven, three [went over] to them;
those who were six, four [went over] to them;
then indeed five were just five.
These five sets of ten excluded these five
from whatever was Manu's own.
Therefore they ran up to Manu himself;
they sought refuge with Manu.
To them he gave these kindling-sticks;
with them they burned them out;
with them they defeated them.
He defeats his evil rival, who, knowing thus, puts on these kindling-sticks.
[With] "You are Agni's kindling-stick;
protect me from curse":
from curse he protects him.
[With] "You are Soma's kindling-stick;
be a supreme protector for me":
he becomes a supreme protector for him.
[With] "You are Yama's kindling-stick;
protect me from death":
from death he protects him.
This, indeed, Nārada used to say:
"This indeed was the attending upon the fire."
From curse he protects him;
he becomes a supreme protector for him;
from death he protects him.

1.5.9.m:
[With] "You, O Agni, are the giver of life;
give life to me":
life indeed he places in him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the giver of splendor;
give splendor to me":
splendor indeed he places in him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of sight;
protect my sight":
sight indeed he protects for him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of hearing;
protect my hearing":
hearing indeed he protects for him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of the body;
protect my body":
the body indeed he protects for him.
[With] "Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me":
whatever indeed of himself is deficient, whatever of offspring, whatever of cattle, that he fills up by this, that he causes to swell.
[With] "O Agni, what heat is yours":
these indeed are Agni's luminous bodies.
This, indeed, Aruṇa Aupaveśi used to say:
"Whichever rivals of myself who are of greater excellence, of greater welfare, I perceive, them let us defeat with these luminous bodies of Agni."
He defeats his evil rival, who, knowing thus, attends upon the fire.
[With] "O Agni, lord of lights, homage to your light;
place light in me":
light [already] pacified he places in himself; he becomes full of fiery energy, endowed with Brahmanical splendor.
[With] "O you whose wealth is wondrous, safely may I reach your other side":
night indeed is Citrāvasu (whose wealth is wondrous);
day is Arvāgvasu (whose wealth is hitherward);
Agni indeed is the night;
yonder sun is the day;
these two indeed are the two halves;
to them he prays.
He speaks thrice;
for the gods have threefold truth.
His sacrifice or the sacred word shines forth, who knows thus.
[With] "You are water;
may I partake of your water":
for they are water.
[With] "You are greatness;
may I partake of your greatness":
for they are greatness.
[With] "You are nourishment;
may I partake of your nourishment":
for they are nourishment.
[With] "You are increase of wealth;
may I partake of your increase of wealth":
for they are increase of wealth.
[With] "O wealthy ones, rest in this womb, in this cowpen":
in their own womb, in their own cowpen he settles them.
[With] "This is your kin;
do not go away from here;
become many;
do not abandon me":
he prays for a blessing.
He touches the calf;
for cattle are fond of the calf;
this one indeed rules over thousandfold increase;
he thrives by a thousand, he does not fall away from a thousand, who knows thus.
[With] "You are united, all-formed":
for with form after form she is united;
with forms indeed he prospers her.
He took their name;
he made friendship with them;
and indeed, when he takes the name of a friend, he makes him a friend thereby.
